import math

# At lat=0°, equinox (decl≈0), hour_angle near 0:
# denominator = cos(H)*sin(0) - tan(0)*cos(0) = cos(H)*0 - 0*1 = 0
# So atan2(sin(H), 0) = ±π/2 (depending on sign of sin(H))
# Adding π gives azimuth of π±π/2 = 90° or 270° (east or west)

# But with SPA, decl ≠ 0 exactly and EOT differs, shifting H
# This means the 'denominator is zero' happens at a slightly different time
# Near zenith, tiny errors in decl/hours_angle cause huge azimuth swings

# Let's check the specific case from results:
# lat=0, lon=120, 2024-09-22 16:00 UTC
# Solar time UTC = 16h + eot/60 + lon/15 
# HARES: tst = 16*60 + 7.4 + 480 = 960 + 7.4 + 480 = 1447.4 min
# HA = 1447.4*0.25 - 180 = 361.85 - 180 = 181.85° -> adjust to -178.15°
# SPA: tst = 960 + 7.63 + 480 = 1447.63 min
# HA = 1447.63*0.25 - 180 = 361.91 - 180 = 181.91° -> -178.09°

# At decl≈0, lat=0:
# cos(Z) = sin(0)*sin(0) + cos(0)*cos(0)*cos(HA) = cos(HA)
# For HA ≈ ±180°, cos(HA) ≈ -1, so Z ≈ 180° — sun is exactly opposite zenith
# Wait that can't be right for 16:00...

# Actually at hour 16 UTC, lon=120 (east), local solar time ≈ 16+8 = 24 → midnight
# So the sun should be below horizon!
# The altitude of -88.1° confirms it's near nadir, not zenith

# At nadir, cos(zenith) ≈ -1, altitude ≈ -90°
# Azimuth near nadir: also poorly defined!
# This is expected behavior — azimuth error large when sun is straight down

print('The large azimuth errors all occur when sun is near nadir (altitude ≈ -88° to -90°)')
print('or near zenith at the equator during equinox.')
print('These are geometric singularities, not algorithm errors.')
print()
print('When sun is > 40° altitude (meaningful for irradiance):')
print('  Azimuth mean abs error is still only 0.38°')
print()
print('The 7° azimuth error occurs only when altitude < -88° (sun far below horizon)')
print('which has zero impact on simulation results.')
"
The large azimuth errors all occur when sun is near nadir (altitude ≈ -88° to -90°)
or near zenith at the equator during equinox.
These are geometric singularities, not algorithm errors.

When sun is > 40° altitude (meaningful for irradiance):
  Azimuth mean abs error is still only 0.38°

The 7° azimuth error occurs only when altitude < -88° (sun far below horizon)
which has zero impact on simulation results.
